# Cloudflare Pages redirects.
# True HTTP 301 for /github → framework repo (PRD §3 / §9). This takes
# precedence over the Astro-generated /github/index.html meta-refresh page,
# which stays as a fallback for any other host. Keep in sync with the
# `redirects` entry in astro.config.mjs.
/github  https://github.com/open-multi-agent/open-multi-agent  301

# Historical product URL still linked from third-party sites. Preserve its
# accumulated signals instead of leaving Search Console with a persistent 404.
/developer  /getting-started/quick-start/  301

# Conventional developer entry points. Agents (and people) probe /docs and
# /developers before they read a nav. /docs lands on the Getting Started index
# rather than a specific page, so the redirect stays right as that section grows.
# These live here rather than in astro.config.mjs `redirects` on purpose: the
# static build turns that config into a meta-refresh stub page, which a non-JS
# client sees as a 200 with no content — a real 301 is the whole point.
/docs        /getting-started/                301
/developers  /getting-started/quick-start/    301

# Campaign short links. Outbound links carry UTM parameters so a channel that
# strips the referrer — messaging apps, in-app webviews, email clients, QR
# codes — is still attributable; these keep that from showing as a wall of
# query string wherever the URL is visible.
#
# 302, unlike every rule above. Those are permanent URL moves; these are not.
# A campaign's destination changes (a new landing page, a new campaign tag),
# and a 301 would be cached by browsers that already followed it — which is
# how the launch-day redirect became uncorrectable. 302 also keeps these out
# of the canonical picture: search engines credit the destination.
#
# Both the bare and trailing-slash forms are listed, because these rules match
# the path exactly and Astro's `trailingSlash: 'always'` does not govern edge
# redirects.
#
# The /go/{me,repo,org} three are GitHub's link fields: the profile sidebar,
# a repository's About panel, and an organization's website. GitHub renders all
# three as the URL itself — the About panel trims the scheme, the other two not
# even that — so a query string sits in the open on pages built to be looked at.
# Verified by reading the rendered pages, not assumed.
#
# Links inside README content do not need these: markdown gives them anchor
# text, so the URL is never shown, and a canonical URL with parameters is
# preferable there — it keeps working if this file ever loses the rule.

/go/devto        /?utm_source=devto&utm_medium=referral       302
/go/devto/       /?utm_source=devto&utm_medium=referral       302
/go/xhs          /?utm_source=xhs&utm_medium=social           302
/go/xhs/         /?utm_source=xhs&utm_medium=social           302
/go/wx           /?utm_source=wx&utm_medium=social            302
/go/wx/          /?utm_source=wx&utm_medium=social            302
# Short link uses the current brand, the UTM value the name analytics tools
# and years of prior data still use.
/go/x            /?utm_source=twitter&utm_medium=social       302
/go/x/           /?utm_source=twitter&utm_medium=social       302
/go/email        /?utm_source=email&utm_medium=email          302
/go/email/       /?utm_source=email&utm_medium=email          302
/go/newsletter   /?utm_source=newsletter&utm_medium=email     302
/go/newsletter/  /?utm_source=newsletter&utm_medium=email     302
/go/yuanasi      /?utm_source=yuanasiweb&utm_medium=referral  302
/go/yuanasi/     /?utm_source=yuanasiweb&utm_medium=referral  302
/go/me           /?utm_source=github&utm_medium=user_profile  302
/go/me/          /?utm_source=github&utm_medium=user_profile  302
/go/repo         /?utm_source=github&utm_medium=repo_about    302
/go/repo/        /?utm_source=github&utm_medium=repo_about    302
/go/org          /?utm_source=github&utm_medium=org_profile   302
/go/org/         /?utm_source=github&utm_medium=org_profile   302
